releases.notes:
Defaults now leave the picture exactly as in vanilla: Nimbus only adds FPS out of the box. Graphics profiles and manual settings are where visible trade-offs live.

- Defaults change nothing visible:
  - entity, block entity, sign text, frame content, name tag and detail distances are "as in the game";
  - fog on, distant terrain (LOD) off, leaves keep their inner faces, no particle cap;
  - existing configs that still had the old defaults move to the new ones; values changed by hand stay.
- Graphics profile (formerly "Sacrifices") applies only with Apply, and Undo brings the old settings back. Before, one click on the arrow switched the whole profile, including vanilla options.
- World resolution below 100% with sharpening is fast again: upscaling and sharpening run in one full-screen pass from the low-resolution image instead of three. On an APU, 80% with sharpening went from slower than 100% to +27% FPS.
- Fast leaves look as in vanilla again: the transparent-texel fill only goes into mipmaps, and it is now an option ("No dark fringes far away"), off by default.
- Settings screen:
  - centered panel that scales with GUI scale; GUI scale applies at once;
  - pages: Main (profile, render distance, graphics, world resolution, FPS limit) / Graphics / Shaders / Objects / Particles / Performance / Advanced (engine and low-level optimizations);
  - long lists are collapsed, dependent settings hide once their parent is off (LOD distance, fog distance, sharpening at 100%, cloud height…);
  - less clutter: no ON/OFF labels, no row lines, arrows on hover, Undo/Apply only with pending changes;
  - Russian and English texts rewritten.
- Resource reloads (packs, language, F3+T) keep the game on screen with a thin progress line instead of the Mojang screen (RRLS-like, off when RRLS is installed).
- Memory, ~26 MB less in a 16-chunk world with the Nimbus engine:
  - vanilla section layer buffers are created only when a layer gets geometry;
  - section neighbour origins are derived on demand;
  - block face shapes and block state cache arrays are shared by content (works next to Lithium too);
  - small NBT compounds no longer reserve 33-slot maps.
- Creative and recipe book search trees are built when those screens first open, not on every world join (Lazy item search).

Nimbus now loads OptiFine/Iris-format shader packs on its own, without Sodium and Iris. With Sodium installed, Sodium + Iris are still used as before.

- Shader packs: Complementary, BSL, Photon, Sildur's Vibrant and Mellow work and match the Iris look. On the test APU, FPS is level with Iris on Complementary, BSL and Photon.
- Supported features:
  - render targets and passes: gbuffers, shadow, deferred/composite/final;
  - compute shaders, custom images, SSBOs (`bufferObject.N`);
  - `block.properties`, `entity.properties`, `item.properties`, and the `entityId` / `blockEntityId` / `currentRenderedItemId` / `heldItemId` uniforms;
  - custom uniforms and textures;
  - `shaders.properties` keys (blend, alphaTest, scale, flip, `shadow.culling` incl. `reversed`, `clouds`/`sun`/`moon`/`vignette`/`underwaterOverlay`, `particles.ordering`);
  - pack options screen with profiles, saved to `shaderpacks/<pack>.txt` like Iris.
- Colored lighting: voxel-based colored lighting works, e.g. Complementary's `COLORED_LIGHTING`. It relies on `at_midBlock` (with block emission in `.w`) and `renderStage` in the shadow pass.
- The shadow pass draws only the sections whose shadow can reach the camera's view: about 1270 → 450 draws, with no visible difference.
- Packs see `IS_IRIS`, `IRIS_VERSION` and the `IRIS_FEATURE_*` flags for the features above, so they take their Iris code paths.
- Not yet supported: Distant Horizons / Voxy programs, `voxelizeLightBlocks`, `separateEntityDraws`, `beacon.beam.depth`, `IRIS_FEATURE_FADE_VARIABLE`.
- Without a shader pack, rendering is unchanged.
